notes: >-
  Three rounds of five isolated finders, fifteen verifiers, then a final audit of the WU
  result report against the tree. Every one of the eighteen verified findings was in code or
  prose this WU itself added, and every one is fixed on this tip - none waived, none deferred.
  Round one found the regression that mattered: BodyViewer passing the fetched byte count as
  the cut marker's total diverges from the shown slice's re-encoded count for a body that is
  not valid UTF-8, printing "showing 384 KiB of 133 KiB" - shown greater than total, which
  inverts the one statement CutMarker exists to make. Round two found the first fix's own
  guard was a proxy: it read a replacement character in the shown slice as proof of a lossy
  decode, but a body can carry that character on purpose, so a valid body with a free accurate
  size was charged a whole-body encode - the false positive landing on exactly the mis-encoded
  traffic this proxy exists to reveal. The fact moved to decodeBody, where the decoder answers
  it rather than a reader guessing. Round two also found the memo enforcing its cap on lookups
  as well as inserts, an untyped memo key on which a numeric string collides with the number
  moment reads differently, a comment claiming the cache holds only what is on screen when
  nothing evicts a scrolled-away row, a memo-miss bench whose purity rested on warm-up
  happening to consume an overlapping pool, and a report claiming every before number came
  from a run at the benchmark's own commit when three of the cases did not exist there. Round
  three found no defect in shipped behaviour and one in the WU's own discipline - the decode
  the second round introduced was changed on a claim about its cost and measured only
  afterwards (whole 388.98 hz against the lenient decode's 379.15, so the claim held; failed
  9.55 ms against 2.64, which is the fallback's real price, now recorded) - plus a factor
  contradicting the table two lines beneath it and three comments narrating the work rather
  than stating what holds. The final audit found seven further stale claims in the report,
  all the same mistake: the numbers table was corrected and the prose quoting it was not.
  Ten refusals are worth recording because they were settled by evidence rather than argument.
  DEC-16 expressly refuses rejected-alternative rationale in a component's TSDoc, so leaving
  FormRenderer's untouched was the rule, not an omission. hostOf cannot silently diverge from
  toRow because a test pins them across a table of URI shapes, and toRow needs a full parse
  for its path regardless. A BOM-prefixed body was raised as a defect and refused in the
  opposite direction: a save writes the mark, so the bytes' length is the correct total and
  the decoded text's would undercount - the invariant only widens. And the blocker raised
  against the benchmark's warm memo was refuted for the case it names, because a flow's
  started_at never changes, so an unscrolled re-render genuinely is all hits.
  Gate 0 is green on this tip. It was red on the previous session's attempt, on two SSH-signing
  tests in files this diff does not touch, reproducible at the base commit and caused by macOS
  Directory Services failing to resolve the uid so ssh-keygen could not run; no evidence was
  written until the machine recovered and the trio passed on this exact unchanged tree.
design:
  undesigned:
    - path: ui/src/BodyViewer.tsx
      why: >-
        Its text-kind dispatch decodes through decodeBody and passes the fetched byte count only
        where the decode read every byte. No surface is shaped: the same renderer receives the
        same text, one further prop carries a number the fetch already held, and the pane's own
        assertions pass untouched. The edit routes a fact to the component that reports it.
    - path: ui/src/components/TextRenderer.tsx
      why: >-
        Gained an optional size prop, and a TSDoc line stating the contract a caller must honour
        to pass it, so the cut marker's total is taken rather than re-derived by encoding the
        whole body. Rendered output is identical for every body whose decode read every byte,
        and the seven assertions that predate this WU pass unchanged. A byte count is arithmetic,
        not a design question.
    - path: ui/src/components/TimestampText.tsx
      why: >-
        The formatted string is remembered under the day it was rendered under, and today's local
        bounds answer the day and year tests by comparison instead of by building two moments per
        render. All four of DEC-22's forks - absolute, seconds, local, 24h - are preserved and
        re-asserted by three added boundary tests, and the eleven assertions that predate this WU
        pass unchanged. Only cost moved.
